   \"signature\": trial[\"signature\"],\n            \"success\": trial[\"success\"]\n        }\n        memory.append(item)\n        if len(memory) > capacity:\n            memory.pop(0)  # FIFO\n        # Collect unique signatures in order\n        sig = trial[\"signature\"]\n        if sig not in seen_set:\n            seen_set.add(sig)\n            seen_signatures.append(sig)\n    return {\n        \"memory\": memory,\n        \"signatures\": seen_signatures\n    }\n```\n\nBut note: The memory items after processing will have last_trial set to the original index, even if they are later evicted? Actually, we only keep the last capacity items. The items that are evicted are removed. So the memory at the end contains items with indices from the tail of the trial list. That's fine.\n\nHowever, is last_trial the index in the original trials or the index in the memory? If it's the memory index, we'd reassign after eviction. But we don't. So it's the original index.\n\nWe need to ensure the output strictly matches the schema: no extra keys. Our items have exactly the four required keys.\n\nNow we need to output the implementation as a JSON string with key \"implementation\".\n\nWe'll write the Python source with proper escaping for JSON.\n\nAlso, note that the \"signatures\" array should be strings. Our seen_signatures are strings.\n\nThus, final answer: a JSON object {\"implementation\": \"<escaped Python code>\"}.\n\nWe'll produce that.\n\nDouble-check: The eval says \"Return one JSON object with exactly one key, `implementation`. Its value must be Python source defining `solve(case)`.\" So we should not include import statements? The function can use standard library, no imports needed. So we can just define the function.\n\nMake sure to escape newlines and quotes.\n\nWe'll write the code as a single line? We'll use triple quotes? But inside JSON string, we need to escape newlines as \\n and quotes as \\\". We'll format the code with indentation and then escape.\n\n